From: LEVY%BESSIE@venus.cis.yale.edu
Subject: Writing Systems (was Human Language

Phil Fraering writes:

>Uh, Simon!, when was the discovery made that all writing had
>semitic roots?
>
>As far as I know, the phoenicians never made it to Korea...
>
>Please explain, I'm sure I'm only confused on this.

The point I was making is that the *alphabetic* concept -- that you can 
represent a single sound (instead of a syllable, whole word, or idea) with
a single symbol -- was probably only discovered once, by speakers of a
West Semitic language.  There are six likely independent writing systems, some
of which are mixed phonographic (sound-based) and logographic (idea-based),
such as Chinese, and some of which are purely phonographic (e.g., West 
Semitic), but it was only in one of West Semitic systems, possibly the one 
used by the Phoenicians, that someone made the discovery that you can anlalyze
language into units smaller than a syllable.  From this discovery, the 
alphabetic concept was spread by scholarship, trade (for which the Phoenicians 
were famous), and other cultural interaction.  According to my former advisor, 
Ignatius Mattingly (1992), "Germanic runes and Korean alphabetic writing 
probably belong to this tradition also, though the derivations are not clear."
I presume that the Korean scholar who invented the Korean alphabet was (like 
most scholars in Asia) trained in Buddhism, and hence knew about alphabets by 
his familiarity with the alphabet of Sanskrit, the language of the oldest 
Buddhist texts.

-- Simon!

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Reference:

Mattingly, I.G. (1992) "Linguistics Awareness and Orthographic Form."  In
	R. Frost and L. Katz, eds., _Orthography, Phonology, Morphology, and
	Meaning_.  North-Holland: Elsevier.



