>From Duncan Frissell:

One article on GATT in the Economist contains *much* more information 
on (and understanding of) GATT than watching CNN and listening to 
National Commie Radio full time for two years.

This is because writing is *coding*.  The boiled down and compressed 
product of one or more human minds thinking over the course of
minutes, months, or years.
.....................................

When one reads another's writing, one gets a picture of the world as 
seen through *their* eyes, and their translation of events,  whereas 
when one watches say, a video of an event or listens to interviews 
(from different sources, of course) of those who are involved with a 
subject, one is constructing more original conclusions about the 
individuals involved and the explanation for the course of events.

There is a beneficial advantage in being able to get information from 
another's work of comprehending a subject, but it should not be 
neglected the importance as well of the exercise of one's original 
judgement upon a subject; of developing one's analytical, perceptual 
abilities not only from what is pre-packaged into an intelligible form, 
but also from being able to identify, distinguish, or recognize 
directly the unprocessed data which confronts one's senses & faculties 
of thought.

