Elias Israel writes:

> Arthur Hlavaty writes:
> > 	To me, being "fully human" means having the entire range of
> > human experience, including death.  No, thanks.
> 
> Well, I suppose you could look at it that way, but I was thinking more
> along the lines of Nitschze's Ubermensch. There are all sorts of
> powers, abilities, knowledge, and heroism available to a human. Most
> people don't approach even a small fraction of them.

A word on Nietzsche. He is, in my less than humble opinion, the most
Extropian of all philosophers.

The ubermensch idea ("superman") was influential to many in the early
part of the century, from George Bernard Shaw to comic books to
Hitler. In particular, the use of Nietzsche's writings by the Nazis
has given the impression to many that Nietzsche was some sort of
ur-Nazi.

Nietzsche may have been partially anti-Semitic....though evidence is
strong that his sister, who was anti-Jewish, shaped the impression the
world had after her brother's death in 1900. In any case, what being
anti-Semitic meant in the 1880s is a far cry from what it meant in the
1930s and 40s. And nowhere in Nietzsche's writings can I find any
mention of destroying the Jews or, indeed, any other group.

In fact, whether you agree with him or not in all his views (and who
could?), he was a profoundly gifted and original writer and was deeply
anti-authoritarian. Read "Also Sprach Zarathustra" (the work that
inspired the Richard Strauss piece used in "2001").

Nietzsche was a Promethean, with visions of the ultimate transcendance
of humanity.

My top candidate for the Extropian Hall of Fame.

--Tim May


