Date: Wed, 15 Dec 93 20:49:08 GMT
From: Robin Barooah <extropy@ganesh.demon.co.uk>
Subject: Drug question

X-Mailer: ArcBM 1.11

In message #93-12-266 Kennita Watson writes:

> I have heard from one person who said they didn't like their
> experience with marijuana because in the morning they couldn't
> remember what had happened the previous night.  Has anyone else had
> such an experience, with marijuana or any of the psychedelics?

I tend to find that the more marijuana I use on any given occasion, the less
detail I can remember it in.  When I have used particularly large amounts, my
memory has been little more than a blur.  I have not felt as though I have
'lost' the space of time, as I have with alcohol sometimes - just that the
time while stoned was fuzzy and unclear.

With psilocybin, LSD, and MDMA, I have not experienced any noticable loss of
memory. It can seem hard to describe the experience satisfactorily, but I
generally have no trouble in recalling events and even conversations in the
same detail as normal.  I have had no trouble in later remembering music
which I have heard under the influence of these drugs.

There is evidence for state-dependency in memory; things which you encounter
when under the influence of a drug are subsequently recalled better under the
influence of the same drug.  This has been shown to be the case for alcohol,
and is probably true for marijuana.  Perhaps if the person you mention got
stoned again, they might remember something of the previous time :)

Regular use of marijuana leads to reduction in memory (and therefore
cognitive) performance.  Often this is referred to a problem with short term
memory but my experience leads me to believe that it is a disruption of the
incorporation of short term memory into longer term storage.  

Robin - having given up marijuana (again) :-)

